-590c-88d0-9d5bf110139d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104208" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/2499715807dedd0234363454dea33afb592d497005c4f643b03145b6c89cc222.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $2$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104208/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104204/selection/6ee5e195cd5d2ab32c24ab13c8c717d26372da7ef3ff3aa76c3af815d99987ca" :answer/id #uuid "df95055e-53af-5995-9bc4-42f0837eb3cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104204/selection/c48f8f72539da0c24f0439816f29229d8c3481d7d40b93b30ef9b84286e510f2" :answer/id #uuid "f97b6bc3-f71a-5f4b-bec7-30d94af277e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104204/selection/590244a3337166cd6e8b7e462d4c8f249562c92c298d8d9ceec